1
00:00:01,970 --> 00:00:06,760
Imagine you are an astronaut, you are hundreds of kilometres above Earth,

2
00:00:07,130 --> 00:00:09,760
aboard the International Space Station.

3
00:00:10,270 --> 00:00:12,180
Suddenly, something catches fire.

4
00:00:12,870 --> 00:00:15,360
On Earth, we know what fire looks like.

5
00:00:15,950 --> 00:00:19,920
Hot air rises, flames stretch upwards, we know how to respond.

6
00:00:20,150 --> 00:00:23,640
But in space, there is no familiar up or down.

7
00:00:23,950 --> 00:00:26,280
The flame doesn't behave the way we expect.

8
00:00:26,810 --> 00:00:29,630
It can become almost spherical, burn differently

9
00:00:29,650 --> 00:00:34,960
and even continue burning under conditions where we might expect it to go out.

10
00:00:35,350 --> 00:00:37,020
And here is the frightening part.

11
00:00:37,350 --> 00:00:40,800
You can't simply evacuate a spacecraft when there is a fire.

12
00:00:41,490 --> 00:00:46,180
The people inside depend on us understanding exactly how fire behaves.

13
00:00:47,010 --> 00:00:48,140
So here is our question.

14
00:00:48,570 --> 00:00:51,410
How can we make better fire safety decisions

15
00:00:51,430 --> 00:00:57,680
when the science itself is scattered, difficult to compare, and inaccessible to most people?

16
00:00:59,130 --> 00:01:04,560
NASA has conducted incredible experiments to understand fire in space.

17
00:01:04,950 --> 00:01:08,460
From experiments aboard the ISS to the Saffire missions,

18
00:01:09,130 --> 00:01:11,420
researchers have collected valuable evidence.

19
00:01:11,850 --> 00:01:13,340
But there is a problem.

20
00:01:13,830 --> 00:01:18,040
The evidence is spread across different missions, reports, and experiments.

21
00:01:18,630 --> 00:01:23,120
One experiment records airflow, another records oxygen concentration,

22
00:01:23,410 --> 00:01:26,350
another may leave important conditions unreported.

23
00:01:26,370 --> 00:01:32,060
It's like trying to compare two experiments when neither one is speaking the same language.

24
00:01:32,410 --> 00:01:37,170
We have the evidence, but we need a better way to connect it, understand it,

25
00:01:37,190 --> 00:01:38,300
and explore it.

26
00:01:38,810 --> 00:01:40,860
And that's where Flammé comes in.

27
00:01:41,850 --> 00:01:46,840
Introducing Flammé developed by Team Flammé from the University of Dhaka.

28
00:01:47,190 --> 00:01:48,660
Our vision is simple.

29
00:01:48,910 --> 00:01:53,510
Make space fire research understandable, comparable, and accessible.

30
00:01:53,530 --> 00:01:56,480
And we started by building two things,

31
00:01:56,990 --> 00:01:59,330
a common language for describing experiments

32
00:01:59,350 --> 00:02:05,680
and an interactive laboratory where anyone can explore how fire behaves in space.

33
00:02:07,430 --> 00:02:09,140
First we asked ourselves,

34
00:02:09,530 --> 00:02:14,220
what if every space fire experiment could be described using the same set of conditions?

35
00:02:14,950 --> 00:02:18,510
So we created a standard catalogue of 10 conditions

36
00:02:18,530 --> 00:02:24,140
covering everything from material being burned to oxygen, airflow, gravity,

37
00:02:24,310 --> 00:02:27,260
ignition, and suppression.

38
00:02:30,150 --> 00:02:35,420
This gives us a foundation for comparing experiments consistently.

39
00:02:35,810 --> 00:02:39,440
But we didn't want people to just read about microgravity fire.

40
00:02:39,670 --> 00:02:41,960
We wanted them to experience it.

41
00:02:42,150 --> 00:02:45,340
So we built an interactive 3D flame laboratory.

42
00:02:46,030 --> 00:02:46,830
Let's take a look.

43
00:02:46,850 --> 00:02:51,590
Look at that.

44
00:02:51,760 --> 00:02:56,910
Instead of the familiar candle-like flame, we see a much more spherical flame.

45
00:02:57,140 --> 00:02:59,950
As gravity increases, the flame changes shape.

46
00:03:00,560 --> 00:03:03,090
Now the flame begins to weaken.

47
00:03:04,020 --> 00:03:07,590
And we can explore how different suppression agents affect the flame.

48
00:03:07,700 --> 00:03:11,630
Every change helps explain how different conditions can influence combustion.

49
00:03:12,220 --> 00:03:16,900
Our current flame behaviour is illustrative, not a computational fluid dynamics simulation.

50
00:03:16,920 --> 00:03:24,250
It demonstrates expected trends rather than claiming to reproduce NASA experimental measurements.

51
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:29,820
But this is our starting point, making an otherwise inaccessible scientific phenomenon

52
00:03:29,840 --> 00:03:32,190
something people can actually explore.

53
00:03:34,160 --> 00:03:36,570
But Flammé is bigger than a 3D simulation.

54
00:03:36,900 --> 00:03:41,200
Imagine a researcher comparing two materials before choosing one for a spacecraft.

55
00:03:41,220 --> 00:03:46,580
Imagine an engineer asking whether a material that extinguishes itself on Earth might

56
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:50,190
continue burning under gentle airflow in microgravity.

57
00:03:50,880 --> 00:03:55,540
Imagine a student in Bangladesh exploring a phenomenon that they would otherwise never

58
00:03:55,560 --> 00:03:56,820
have access to.

59
00:03:56,840 --> 00:03:58,750
That's the impact we want to create.

60
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:02,630
Our next step is to turn Flammé into an evidence-driven research platform.

61
00:04:03,240 --> 00:04:06,620
We plan to build a source-linked database of NASA experiments,

62
00:04:06,640 --> 00:04:09,580
compare their conditions, identify gaps in existing research,

63
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:15,090
and eventually ground our flame model in real experimental data.

64
00:04:15,360 --> 00:04:20,700
We also want to publish our standard reporting format so future experiments can contribute

65
00:04:20,720 --> 00:04:22,750
evidence in a consistent way.

66
00:04:24,200 --> 00:04:28,180
Space exploration is about going where humanity has never gone before.

67
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:30,890
But going further also means preparing for dangers

68
00:04:31,260 --> 00:04:32,750
we cannot afford to misunderstand.

69
00:04:33,640 --> 00:04:36,110
Fire doesn't follow our intuition in space.

70
00:04:36,240 --> 00:04:38,460
And safety cannot depend on intuition alone.

71
00:04:38,480 --> 00:04:42,210
We are team Flammé from the University of Dhaka.

72
00:04:42,980 --> 00:04:46,590
Understanding fire beyond Earth to protect the people who explore it.

73
00:04:46,860 --> 00:04:47,350
Thank you.
